Your implementation looks good and should efficiently find the unique words in Text1 that do not appear in Text2 using MATLAB. You're reading each text file, converting each word to lowercase, and then splitting the text into words. After that, you're leveraging the `setdiff` function to find the unique words between the two texts. Finally, the unique words are displayed using the `disp` function.

For a large amount of text, this implementation should work well. If you encounter memory issues while dealing with very large text files, you can try reading the files line by line, using functions like `fgetl` in a loop, in order to prevent loading the entire files into memory at once.

Here's a modified implementation to handle extremely large text files:

```MATLAB
% Open the text files for line by line reading
fid1 = fopen('Text1.txt', 'r');
fid2 = fopen('Text2.txt', 'r');

% Initialize the word sets
words1 = [];
words2 = [];

% Read lines from Text1 and process words
line = fgetl(fid1);
while ischar(line)
    words1 = [words1, lower(strsplit(line))];
    line = fgetl(fid1);
end

% Read lines from Text2 and process words
line = fgetl(fid2);
while ischar(line)
    words2 = [words2, lower(strsplit(line))];
    line = fgetl(fid2);
end

% Close the text files
fclose(fid1);
fclose(fid2);

% Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);

% Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);
```

This will minimize the amount of memory used while processing the text files. Note that this will still create the `words1` and `words2` variables in memory, so if these are too large, you may need to come up with a more advanced approach to handle such cases (e.g., using `containers.Map` with keys based on the hash of each word).

